"Low"}, {"time_utc": "08:30", "currency": "USD", "title": "Prelim Wholesale Inventories m/m", "impact": "Low"}], "editorial": "EUR/USD sits at 1.13759 as Monday's session draws toward its European midpoint, trading within a compressed range that has characterised the pair for the better part of the past week. The daily chart tells a clear story of distribution following a failure to sustain the mid-July push toward 1.1482. Since that high-water mark, the pair has printed a sequence of lower closes, and the last four daily candles have all settled below 1.1400 \u2014 a level that had previously acted as a gravitational centre throughout much of the month.\n\nThe structure story is one of a market that ran hard into layered resistance and has since struggled to reclaim it. The prior reaction zone clustered around 1.1436\u20131.1446 absorbed multiple tests across late June and early July, and it was precisely that band that capped the recovery attempt on 15 July before price began its measured retreat. The daily chart now shows the pair compressing just above the 1.1363 support zone, a prior reaction area that has registered three meaningful touches. That level is the immediate structural floor; a sustained daily close beneath it would represent a meaningful deterioration in the near-term picture and open the conversation toward deeper retracement territory.\n\nOn the H1 timeframe, the intraday read reinforces the bearish lean. Price spent the overnight session drifting steadily lower from the 1.1417 area, with sellers stepping in on each modest bounce attempt. The Asian and early European hours have produced a tight consolidation between roughly 1.1367 and 1.1380, with no meaningful upside momentum materialising. The hourly candles are small-bodied and directionally indecisive in isolation, but the broader context \u2014 sitting at the lower end of the recent range, unable to reclaim 1.1400 \u2014 keeps the near-term read skewed to the downside. Any recovery that stalls beneath 1.1400 would be consistent with the current structure.\n\nToday's calendar is relatively light on high-impact catalysts for EUR/USD directly. The Spanish Unemployment Rate and the German Bundesbank Monthly Report are unlikely to shift the macro narrative materially. On the US side, the CB Consumer Confidence print at 10:00 UTC carries medium-impact status and represents the most meaningful scheduled risk of the session. A notably weak reading could provide a short-term lift to the pair, while a resilient number would likely reinforce dollar demand and add downside pressure. The ADP weekly employment and trade data are lower-tier releases but could contribute to intraday volatility in thin conditions.\n\nWhat would change the read? A decisive hourly close back above 1.1400, followed by a daily settlement above the 1.1436\u20131.1446 prior reaction zone, would materially weaken the bearish case and suggest the recent pullback was corrective rather than the beginning of a more sustained move lower. Equally, a clean break and close beneath 1.1363 support would be the event that validates the current directional bias with greater conviction.\n\nThis is editorial analysis, not financial advice. Trading involves substantial risk of loss.", "sample": false}